uestion-capture/14073156/state.json" "file_sha256" "ab64f97af3f5a9f65f4f02ce5e9cc0cb75986bac92ada0bda3cc81f6df879178"} {"question" "q-126752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(0,1,3)$ and $Q(3,-4,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073156/state.json" "file_sha256" "ab64f97af3f5a9f65f4f02ce5e9cc0cb75986bac92ada0bda3cc81f6df879178"} {"question" "q-126752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=p+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(0,1,3)$ and $Q(3,-4,-2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =⟨3,-4,-2⟩-⟨0,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-5,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073156/state.json" "file_sha256" "ab64f97af3f5a9f65f4f02ce5e9cc0cb75986bac92ada0bda3cc81f6df879178"} {"question" "q-126752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073156/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4aa45eff4688d5db2e903a0785eccff8ba8ea57ae78058a7e274d455c59811b"} {"question" "q-126752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/questi